00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.929 align:start position:0%
EU economics and finance ministers have  made progress towards completing an anti  abuse clause to stop multinational  companies avoiding tax payments the EU  so called parent-subsidiary directive  was adopted in 2011 to ensure that  profits made by cross-border groups are  not taxed twice and anti-abuse clause is  needed to prevent companies from  exploiting any tax loopholes The  ministers are expected to finalize the  claws at their next council in early  December we are now very well placed to  reach such a definite agreement at the  echofon meeting in December especially  in the current context that we all know  there is no time to waste in reducing  any opportunities for abusive practices  and aggressive tax planning the  ministers also made progress on a  financial transactions tax that eleven  EU member states want to introduce to  ensure that the financial sector makes a  fair contribution to tax revenues they  expressed hope that it could be  finalised by the end of this year and  into force in 2016 in tandem with the  European Commission The ministers  debated a compromise on the revised  gross national income and v8t figures  which would require some countries  notably the United Kingdom Italy and the  Netherlands to pay substantial sums of  money into the EU budget by the first of  December the Commission's been asked to  come up with a proposal to allow  countries to make such payments in  stages when the amount is exceptionally  high as it is this time around in other  business the council committed to  mobilize more finances to help fight  climate change ahead of next month's UN  climate talks in Lima Peru
 

